%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8277db26963c4a7a81a4dcb5e6307197, type: 3}
  m_Name: BendingQuiz
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  quizId: civil-craft-bending-quiz-v1
  title: Bending Quiz
  assessmentMode: 1
  questions:
  - questionId: bending
    prompt: What is <b><#E09500>Bending</color></b> in bridge construction?
    choices:
    - text: The movement of a bridge from one location to another.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: The expansion of bridge materials due to temperature.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: The curving or deformation of a structural member under an applied load.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: The complete removal of forces acting on a bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    correctChoiceIndex: 2
    feedback: Bending is the curving or deformation of a structural member under an applied load.
  - questionId: vehicle-load
    prompt: What happens when a vehicle crosses a bridge?
    choices:
    - text: Its weight creates a downward load on the structure.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: Its weight removes the forces acting on the bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: Its weight only affects the ground beneath the bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: Its weight prevents structural members from bending.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    correctChoiceIndex: 0
    feedback: Vehicle weight creates a downward load that the bridge transfers toward its supports.
  - questionId: maximum-bending
    prompt: For a simply supported bridge with a vehicle positioned at the middle, where does the greatest bending moment commonly occur?
    choices:
    - text: At the bridge entrance.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: At the bridge supports.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: Beyond the end of the bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: Near the middle of the bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    correctChoiceIndex: 3
    feedback: In this simply supported case, a load at midspan commonly produces the greatest bending moment near the middle.
  - questionId: compression-and-tension
    prompt: When a simply supported beam bends downward under a vehicle's weight, what happens to its upper and lower parts?
    choices:
    - text: Both parts experience only compression.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: The upper part experiences compression, while the lower part experiences tension.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: The upper part experiences tension, while the lower part experiences compression.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: Both parts experience no internal forces.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    correctChoiceIndex: 1
    feedback: In downward bending of a simply supported beam, the upper part is compressed and the lower part is stretched in tension.
  - questionId: structural-support
    prompt: Why is understanding bending important when designing a bridge?
    choices:
    - text: It eliminates the need to test the bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: It ensures that no loads will act on the bridge.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: It helps identify areas that may need additional structural support.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    - text: It allows the bridge to carry any weight without limitations.
      image: {fileID: 0}
      imageFingerprint:
    correctChoiceIndex: 2
    feedback: Understanding bending helps identify areas needing more support; testing still confirms whether the bridge safely carries its intended load.
